## Proposed hierarchy

The owner proposed a Code Review Orchestrator with Standards Compliance, Forward Compatibility, Integrations & Dependencies and Wiki Impact responsibilities. The expanded structure below is the implementation proposal, including an explicit behavior/verification lens and separate repair workers.

```text
Mission Control
└── Review and Merge — existing mc-review-and-merge
    ├── Intake, integration-unit assessment and recovery checkpoint
    ├── Initial Code Review Orchestrator — proposed subordinate manager
    │   ├── Fresh Behavior & Verification reviewer
    │   ├── Fresh Standards Compliance reviewer
    │   ├── Fresh Integrations & Dependencies reviewer
    │   ├── Fresh Forward Compatibility reviewer
    │   ├── Fresh Wiki Impact reviewer
    │   └── Fresh conditional specialist(s), when the change warrants them
    ├── Bounded implementation worker(s)
    │   └── Manager-assigned fresh worker-handoff review
    ├── Bounded Wiki update worker
    │   └── Manager-assigned fresh documentation-handoff review
    ├── Final Code Review Orchestrator — fresh final evaluation
    │   └── Fresh reviewers covering the complete final candidate
    ├── Development app launch and runtime verification
    ├── Owner checkpoint — waiting-owner, ready to inspect and commit
    └── Only authorized publication and integration receipts
```

Review managers coordinate evidence and findings. Reviewers inspect; implementation and Wiki workers edit their assigned files. Review managers do not certify edits they authored. Workers do not acquire authority to launch wider builds, manage Mission Control or approve publication.

These are responsibilities and proposed execution roles, not a claim that every child profile is installed. A separate profile for each review lens is probably unnecessary: the existing clean-room reviewer can receive a precise lens-specific assignment. A reusable Code Review Orchestrator and a bounded integration worker may provide the needed separation. Profile names and packaging remain implementation choices for the main chat.

The existing roadmap hierarchy stays separate. Planning worker-handoff, stage and release validation do not certify executable code. The Implementation Supervisor's final-roadmap review remains necessary in its own workflow; the combined integration candidate still requires this integration review.

## Intake and recoverable checkpoint

When an integration request arrives, establish the job before making repairs. Record accepted work IDs, whether the integration unit contains one or several SPECs, source and target baselines, prerequisite evidence, assigned checkout, writer ownership and the exact scope of permitted changes.

A checkpoint is possible without creating a Git commit. Prefer preparing the candidate in an isolated checkout or preview while preserving the source checkout and shared target. Save a recovery manifest and the assigned input preimages before preparation. Include tracked dirty input, relevant untracked input and any existing staged changes that affect the candidate; a HEAD SHA alone cannot identify uncommitted work.

The recovery manifest should identify:

- Repository root, actual checkout/worktree path, branch and HEAD.
- Source commit IDs, target revision and applicable SPEC/decision revisions.
- Owned paths and the existing dirty/staged/untracked state within the assignment.
- Recoverable patch/copy locations, including binary files where applicable.
- Input fingerprints and the method used to reproduce the candidate.
- Other active writers or unrelated changes that must be preserved.
- Runtime data used for testing and whether a separate fixture/profile snapshot exists.
- The safe restoration procedure and its limits.

Do not use an ordinary merge, cherry-pick, rebase or “checkpoint commit” before the owner's commit authorization. Some Git operations create commits indirectly. Preparation must also avoid advancing the shared target branch.

Recovery means restoring the job's owned candidate to its recorded state, or abandoning an isolated preview while preserving its report. It does not mean resetting a shared checkout and erasing another writer's changes. If concurrent changes prevent safe restoration, stop and report the collision rather than force a reset.

Code recovery and runtime-data recovery are different. A code checkpoint does not undo database migrations, external side effects or test writes to a real profile. Use authorized isolated fixtures where needed and record any additional recovery requirement before those effects occur. Preserve the development and Alpha databases; never imply that a patch backup protects them.

## Initial review lenses and ordering

First establish the candidate identity, authority, baseline and scope. Then review independent concerns in parallel where the runtime and assignment permit it. Review findings are consolidated before repair workers receive assignments.

| Lens | Primary question | Expected evidence |
|---|---|---|
| Behavior & Verification | Does the combined candidate produce the intended behavior, and are the important failure and regression paths checked? | Accepted outcomes, relevant code paths, test results, meaningful gaps and reproducible failures. |
| Standards Compliance | Does the implementation meet applicable code standards and owner design heuristics? | Specific rules, module boundaries, consistency with shared mechanisms and concrete maintainability consequences. |
| Integrations & Dependencies | Are contracts, ordering, persistence and consumers compatible on the proposed target? | Changed interfaces, producers/consumers, migrations, conflict resolutions, blast radius and prerequisite release conditions. |
| Forward Compatibility | Does this design obstruct or complicate actual known future work? | Accepted plans, established extension points, specific foreseeable incompatibility and the cost of leaving it unresolved. |
| Wiki Impact | What documentation becomes inaccurate or incomplete, and what needs to change? | Canonical pages, source references, changed behavior, existing workflows and documentation obligations. |

Conditional specialists should be commissioned when a concrete risk warrants them: security/trust boundaries, persistence/migration safety, performance/resource lifecycle, packaging or another affected domain. They should have bounded questions rather than a standing mandate to audit the whole application.

Behavior & Verification is a proposed addition to the owner's four named lenses. Without it, a candidate could satisfy standards and documentation expectations while failing the intended user behavior.

Forward Compatibility must distinguish known upcoming work from hypothetical future uses. It should identify brittleness created by real plans without inventing abstractions or implementing future features as part of review.

Wiki Impact can identify obligations early. Final page edits should follow settled code repairs so the Wiki describes the final candidate. Dependent review work should not pretend to be independent merely to maximize parallelism.

## Applying heuristics and disposing of deviations

Routine technical deviations should be resolved by the agent using the owner's established intent and heuristics. Record the original SPEC expectation, the implemented correction, why it better satisfies the governing intent, and any dependency or verification effect. Reporting a deviation does not automatically make it an owner approval request.

The working preference is to resolve evidenced avoidable debt now when the assignment can safely do so. Reuse established mechanisms; fix known contract weaknesses and misleading documentation; avoid creating a brittle seam that actual upcoming work will immediately have to undo.

A deferral needs a concrete reason. Suitable evidence could be a named approved SPEC that owns the same correction, a prerequisite that makes a safe fix impossible now, or a boundary where the desired behavior is not yet decided. Include the responsible work item and observable release condition. “Someone should improve this later” is not a disposition.

Autonomy has a boundary. If a repair requires choosing previously unspecified product behavior, incompatible user semantics, a different architectural direction or a materially expanded integration unit, surface that issue. The main agent should recognize missing intent itself; it must not rely exclusively on a reviewer to find it.

Advisory recommendations can remain without blocking a gate when they are speculative or optional. Concrete violations of accepted standards, known intent or necessary compatibility obligations are material. Make the owner's debt preference part of the review assignment so it is evaluated as an actual requirement, not presented as unsolicited polish.

## Consolidated repair and Wiki updates

The Code Review Orchestrator returns an evidence-backed findings ledger. Review and Merge reconciles overlapping findings and contradictions against source authority, then assigns a coherent repair packet. Do not give workers competing fixes for the same files.

Implementation workers own bounded files and outcomes. They receive relevant sources, applicable heuristics, candidate identity, findings, dependency contracts and verification requirements. They preserve other sessions' changes, self-check their work, record technical deviations and return exact changed-file and verification evidence.

Their substantive outputs require manager-assigned independent handoff review before acceptance. This review asks whether the repair satisfies its specific assignment and whether its report is trustworthy. It does not replace evaluation of the complete final candidate.

If multiple workers are useful, separate their write scopes and coordinate shared seams explicitly. A single worker is preferable when fixes are tightly coupled. The hierarchy supports parallelism where ownership and dependencies permit it; it does not require artificial splitting.

The Wiki worker receives the settled behavior and the Wiki Impact report. Follow current page update and audit rules:

- Preserve a complete preimage under `.versions/` before a substantive page rewrite.
- Maintain current frontmatter and actual UTC modification times on edited pages.
- Update source references and affected cross-links.
- Preserve generated marker blocks; use the documented staged audit/generation procedure.
- Import only intended generated changes, excluding audit runtime state.
- Validate the changed pages and report the actual coverage checked.

Use Sync Wiki Context when changes affect the Wiki Guide's structure or coverage. Its five read-only research assignments and accountable editor are a specific workflow, not a requirement to refresh the entire Guide for every code change. The existing Document Sweep skill reviews Launchpad documentation; it is not a substitute for canonical Wiki editing.

Use the Wiki Update package as the source for its separately assigned research/repair/audit procedures and article rules. Its full supervisor run remains manually invoked; the Review and Merge design does not add automatic Wiki Update dispatch, timers or a post-commit hook. An integration job's bounded Wiki editing/review can follow those procedures without claiming that the separate full Wiki Update run occurred. Once factual change detection exists, its candidate list can inform Wiki Impact; required integration documentation coverage cannot depend solely on declared source mappings or an unknown scan baseline.

## Clean-room gates and repair loop

Use clean-room discipline at distinct junctures, with the question appropriate to each gate:

| Gate | What it establishes |
|---|---|
| Initial review | The proposed integration's defects, obligations and repair needs against its actual authority and baseline. |
| Implementation worker handoff | The bounded repair satisfies its assignment and supplies usable evidence. |
| Wiki worker handoff | Changed documentation accurately describes the settled behavior and follows the applicable Wiki procedure. |
| Final combined-candidate review | The complete repaired code, integration decisions, Wiki changes and verification evidence satisfy the integration unit. |
| Runtime verification | The development app actually runs from the reviewed candidate and supports the owner's acceptance testing. |

The final combined review is independent of the initial reviewers and of the repair workers. It must cover all applicable lenses and changed seams, including documentation. Whether that uses several fresh lens reviewers or a fresh combined reviewer plus risk specialists can be decided from scope, but coverage cannot silently disappear.

Reviewers start fresh without the author's conversation, another reviewer's history or a desired verdict. Give them the original authority, bounded assignment, current candidate and raw evidence. Review managers retain the ledger and reconcile findings after receiving the independent reports.

A material finding should state the violated requirement or necessary missing intent, direct evidence, affected scope and concrete consequence. Preserve raw reviewer output and its disposition. A green self-check is not an independent gate.

After material findings, repair forward and commission fresh review of the repaired scope. Preserve valid unaffected evidence, but recheck changed dependencies and shared seams. A fresh final review must still assess the complete assembled result. Do not manufacture a pass by imposing an arbitrary review-count ceiling. Stop after the first materially clean applicable pass; do not request endless confirmation reviews.

Missing review capability, missing required evidence, unresolved material defects and missing necessary intent are explicit unmet gates. Do not rename them “ready”. Intentional owner or dependency waits must not be restarted as failed runs.

