## Candidate identity and evidence invalidation

Every check and review must identify the candidate it evaluated. Include the target baseline, source revisions, owned changed-file list and reproducible fingerprints for uncommitted content. Record relevant build/test commands, results, logs and skipped checks with reasons.

Changes after review can invalidate evidence: repairs, new source commits, target advancement, generated documentation changes or changed runtime/configuration assumptions. Compare the current candidate and target to the gate's recorded inputs before the owner handoff and again before an authorized publication operation.

Re-evaluate the affected scope when an input changes. Avoid repeating unrelated checks whose validity is preserved, but do not reuse a clean verdict for different bytes or different integration seams. If the owner requests fixes after testing, reopen the repair loop and issue an updated acceptance packet.

## Final development-app handoff

Once code, Wiki and the final review are settled, use the existing Fusion Electron development restart procedure for the intended candidate checkout and machine identity. The procedure rebuilds and starts the development app and server; it is distinct from Alpha deployment.

Verify the actual checkout and runtime identity, the server's startup/health, renderer connection and relevant candidate behavior. Record the server URL, logs, build result and any remaining testing limits. Verify that required generation or startup did not alter reviewed source bytes without a corresponding recheck.

The owner receives a concise “ready to commit” response with:

- What changed and why, including consequential autonomous corrections.
- Where the candidate lives and which app/server is running.
- What to inspect or exercise in the app.
- The completed review/check summary and any remaining advisory notes.
- The detailed report location and recovery checkpoint.
- The exact next action awaiting the owner's decision.

If the app cannot launch correctly, readiness remains unmet. A clean static review cannot substitute for the promised running-app handoff.

The task then enters `waiting-owner` and ends its turn. Do not keep a polling loop active, restart the task for inactivity or treat the owner's deliberation as failure.

## Owner approval, publication and dependencies

The gate precedes **every commit-producing operation and push**. Preparation, clean reviews, Full Access, another agent's recommendation, Mission Control dispatch and an automation wakeup do not supply owner approval.

If the owner says “commit”, follow that scope. Do not infer authority to push, publish a PR, merge it, deploy Alpha or accept the next SPEC. Verify the approved candidate and target are still current before acting; material changes require renewed evidence and an updated owner packet.

If the owner says “fix X and Y”, retain the checkpoint and candidate history, route the corrections, re-evaluate affected evidence and return to the same running-app approval gate.

Where the authorized job includes publication, distinguish local commit, remote push, PR creation, PR merge and actually landed integration in the receipt. Attach a created PR through the existing Codex artifact workflow. After a successful Fusion Studio GitHub push, ask the repository-required Alpha follow-up; do not perform Alpha work without current confirmation.

A completed SPEC is not automatically a complete integration unit. Accepted criteria and dependencies determine whether several SPECs must land together. Conversely, unrelated work should not be held without evidence that it belongs to the same unit.

Only actual landed integration can satisfy a landing dependency. Consumer adoption needs its own evidence. Name the affected roadmap/consumer and release condition; a bulletin entry is not delivered notification. Send messages or create follow-on tasks only under the applicable explicit authority and supported communication rules.

Mission Control's D-023 cycle remains separate: it stops before its next action and on a tracked build completion, including a Review and Merge handoff. This integration workflow does not silently re-arm monitoring or remove a supervisor's per-SPEC owner checkpoint.

## Durable job records and recovery

Use an owned job folder, for example `jobs/review-and-merge/<job-id>/`, under the assigned controller home. This path is a proposal, not an existing live job. Review and Merge owns its job records; Mission Control owns central monitoring and dispatch summaries.

Suggested records are:

| Record | Contents |
|---|---|
| Assignment | Job/work IDs, verified task identity, authority, controller home, product checkout, integration unit, writer scope, prerequisites and report destination. |
| Checkpoint | Source/target identity, input preimages, fingerprints, recovery method, runtime-data limits and preserved unrelated changes. |
| Findings ledger | Finding ID, gate/lens, authority, evidence, consequence, repair owner, disposition, resulting bytes and recheck evidence. |
| Verification log | Exact commands, candidate identity, results, logs, skipped checks and invalidation decisions. |
| Owner packet | Resulting behavior, important changes, reviews, running-app details, recovery point and precisely proposed Git operations. |
| Publication receipt | Owner authorization source, actual commits/destinations/PR, landed baseline, adoption status and unresolved holds. |
| Resume checkpoint | Current phase, active writers, pending gate, last safe state, next action and observable release condition. |

Possible phases include intake, initial-review, repair, wiki-update, final-review, runtime-check, waiting-owner, waiting-dependency, unmet-gate and authorized-publication. These are proposed workflow states, not new central registry values. Choose schema names consistently when implementing.

A successor reads the job packet, establishes whether prior workers are active and confirms the candidate and authority before resuming. It must not duplicate writers, interpret an expected wait as failure or repeat already valid work solely because the previous context filled.

Keep session identity separate from work identity. Use only verified task UUIDs. An ephemeral side chat must not invent one or register itself as the operational Mission Control task.

